  "/fix-blurry-photos": {
    "jsonLd": [
      {
        "data": {
          "@context": "https://schema.org",
          "@type": "FAQPage",
          "mainEntity": [
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "AI restoration can reduce the appearance of mild to moderate motion blur, particularly in facial areas. GFPGAN does not perform deconvolution (the mathematical reversal of motion blur) — instead, it reconstructs plausible facial detail based on learned priors. For severe motion blur where features are completely smeared, results are improved but not perfect.",
              },
              "name": "Can AI fix motion blur in photos?",
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "GFPGAN addresses multiple blur types with different effectiveness: focus blur (soft, out-of-focus faces) — excellent results; film grain and old photo blur — excellent; mild motion blur — good results; severe motion blur — moderate improvement. Compression blur from heavily-compressed JPEGs — good improvement.",
              },
              "name": "Does it work on all types of blur?",
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Standard sharpening (like Unsharp Mask in Photoshop) increases edge contrast without adding new information — it makes an image look sharper but introduces halos and artifacts. AI restoration using GFPGAN actually reconstructs missing facial detail using what it learned from FFHQ, a dataset of 70,000 high-quality face photos. The result is genuinely sharper detail rather than just contrast-enhanced edges.",
              },
              "name": "What is the difference between sharpening and AI restoration?",
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Old scanned photos are blurry for several reasons: low scanner DPI (below 300 creates visible softness), scanner glass not cleaned (dust diffuses light), original film grain (especially ASA 400+ film), and photo paper degradation over time. For best results, rescan at 600 DPI minimum and clean the scanner glass before scanning.",
              },
              "name": "Why are old scanned photos blurry?",
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Magic Memory usually processes a photo in under a minute. The exact time depends on file size and current server load. You will see a progress indicator during processing. There is no quality difference between fast and slow processing — the AI always runs the full restoration model.",
              },
              "name": "How long does it take to fix a blurry photo with AI?",
            },
          ],
        },
