  "/glossary/photo-restoration": {
    "jsonLd": [
      {
        "data": {
          "@context": "https://schema.org",
          "@type": "FAQPage",
          "mainEntity": [
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Photo restoration is the process of repairing and enhancing photographs that have been damaged, degraded, or lost quality over time. It includes fixing scratches, tears, fading, discoloration, blur, and missing detail. Traditional restoration is done manually in software like Photoshop. AI restoration uses neural networks to automate the process.",
              },
              "name": "What is photo restoration?",
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A manual restoration service charges per photo by damage level: as of November 2026, Fixthephoto lists $30, $45 and $60 per photo (plus $12 for each extra person), and FOREVER lists $89.99 per photo. AI tools cost far less per photo: Magic Memory works out at €0.25 to €0.50 per photo with one-time credit packs, but it restores faces and sharpness only, not tears or missing pieces.",
              },
              "name": "How much does professional photo restoration cost?",
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "For blurry, faded or low-resolution faces, AI face restoration models like GFPGAN recover sharp features in under a minute, which is the slowest part of manual work. Manual restoration is still the better choice for torn photos, missing sections, stains across the image, and anything that needs creative reconstruction, because a retoucher can rebuild what the photo no longer shows.",
              },
              "name": "Is AI photo restoration as good as manual restoration?",
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "It depends on the damage. AI restoration can recover faces from severely blurry, faded, or low-resolution photos. However, if facial features are completely obscured — for example, by a tear running through the face or heavy water damage — both AI and manual restoration have limits. The more recognizable the original face, the better the result.",
              },
              "name": "Can photo restoration fix completely destroyed photos?",
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Digitize the print with a high-resolution scan, keep that scan as an untouched master copy, assess the damage, repair it on a copy (dust and scratches, tears, fading and color, then blur and faces), and save the result at full resolution. AI tools automate the face and sharpness step; physical damage still needs a retoucher or Photoshop.",
              },
              "name": "What are the steps of photo restoration?",
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "No. Digital photo restoration works on a scan or a photo of the print, so the original stays untouched. Handle old prints by the edges and scan them flat; do not try to clean or flatten a fragile print yourself.",
              },
              "name": "Does photo restoration damage the original photo?",
            },
          ],
        },
        "sha256": "60e49dcff2d71e2533d99ebcde860b6e8de1f7f2344651383c67384960f2a720",
      },
      {
        "data": {
          "@context": "https://schema.org",
          "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
          "itemListElement": [
            {
              "@type": "ListItem",
